{
  "requirement": "`trust_but_verify::outline::OutlineContent` implements\n`trust_but_verify::domain::NodeContents`, making it usable directly as\n`Domain::NodeContent` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content`.\n\nIts `fingerprint()` returns the selected item's retained structural hash and\nscheme under {cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint`.\nIts `format()` returns the selected source text with recognized credentials\nredacted under {cite}`requirements.sdk.sources.redaction` and\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.format`.\nBoth operations describe the source snapshot retained by the value.\n`text()` retains the original selected bytes for source inspection.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.outlines.node_contents`. Two tests carry `{test}` references to it, and other tests in the same file back them up.\n\n**Obligations derived from the text**\n1. `OutlineContent` implements `NodeContents`, so it can serve as `Domain::NodeContent`.\n2. `fingerprint()` returns the selected item's retained structural hash together with the scheme.\n3. `format()` returns the selected source text with recognized credentials redacted.\n4. Both operations describe the retained snapshot, not the current state of the file.\n5. `text()` retains the original selected bytes.\n\n**Mapping to tests**\n- **Trait implementation (1):**\n  - In `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs`, `fingerprint` and `format` appear only as trait methods on `OutlineContent`, in `impl[OutlineContent/NodeContents]` in `src/outline/contents.rs`. I found no inherent versions of either.\n  - The tests import `trust_but_verify::domain::NodeContents` and call these methods, so the trait implementation is exercised at compile time and at run time.\n  - `tests/contracts.rs` also has a generic `contents<T: NodeContents>` bound check.\n- **Fingerprint (2):**\n  - `fn[selected_contents_outlive_handles_and_later_file_edits]` selects the second line of `first\\nsecond\\n`. It asserts that `fingerprint().value` equals `structural_hash()` and that `fingerprint().scheme` equals `fingerprint_scheme()`.\n  - `fn[explicit_languages_share_configuration_and_retain_their_scheme]` checks the custom scheme `custom-v2` and the selected digest `selected-digest`.\n  - `fn[file_constructors_infer_the_language_and_return_addressed_contents]` checks the scheme `rust-structural-v1`. It also checks that a selected item's fingerprint equals the one from `root.select(...)`.\n  - `fn[structural_fingerprint_example_tracks_the_selected_function]` shows the selected item's fingerprint changes when the function body changes.\n- **Format and redaction (3):**\n  - The first test asserts `format() == \"second\"` for a selected item.\n  - `fn[formatted_contents_redact_credentials_while_retaining_raw_text_and_spans]` asserts several things about a GitHub-token source. `format()` contains `[REDACTED:GitHub Token]` and does not contain the token, and its line count matches the source. A selected item's `format()` is also free of the token.\n  - The same test shows that changing only the credential leaves `fingerprint()` unchanged, which is the redaction-based structural-hash obligation.\n- **Retained snapshot (4):** In the first test, after `drop(file)` and an overwrite of the file with `changed\\n`, `selected.fingerprint()` still equals the earlier value. `format()` and `text()` of the retained selections are unchanged. A fresh `from_id` fails, so the file really did change. The retained contents can still `select(\"\")` the original root text.\n- **Original text (5):** `text()` equals the raw source, including the credential, in the redaction test. The selected item's `text()` still contains the token.\n\n**Gaps considered**\nI found none. The cited `node_content.fingerprint`, `node_content.format` and `sources.redaction` requirements are context. Both tests exercise them for this subject.",
